54-## 3. Orchestration Runtime Model
55-
56-Use this control flow:
57-
58:`Clarify Goal -> Check Single-Agent Sufficiency -> Select Topology -> Define Roles / Contracts / Budgets -> Decide Interaction Mode -> Execute / Coordinate -> Track Lifecycle -> Integrate -> Verify -> Escalate / Recover / Stop`
59-
60-Rules:
61-- do not split work before defining why each split exists
62-- do not create a specialist without a real specialization boundary
63-- do not dispatch work without a return contract or join condition
64-- do not integrate partial outputs whose lifecycle state is unclear
65-- do not let orchestration overhead outgrow the decision value
66-
67:## 4. Activation and Sufficiency Check
68-
69-Before coordinating, answer:
70-- is a single-agent path still sufficient?
71-- what exact gain does orchestration provide?
72-- is the gain in quality, latency, fault isolation, reuse, or controllability?
73-- what coordination cost is introduced?
74-- what failure surface becomes larger?
75-
76-Activation rule:
77:- choose orchestration only on material control gain
78-- if the path becomes obviously single-agent-sufficient, collapse back to the simpler route
79-
80-## 5. Topology Selection Discipline
