{"scenario_id":"EH-S03","bundle_id":"orchestration_sufficiency_bundle","verdict":"Pass","guide_alignment":"aligned","evidence_strength":"direct","operator_only_dependency":false,"over_orchestration":false,"speculative_patch_widening":false,"missing_operational_artifact_route":false,"recommended_patch_needed":false,"minimal_patch_targets":[],"evidence_files":["snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/AGENTS.md","snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/01_base/PROMPT_full.md","snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/02_overlays/PROMPT_multi_agent_overlay.md","snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/codex/CODEX_RUNTIME_GUIDE.md","snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/codex/skills/orchestration-control/SKILL.md","snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/03_examples/PROMPT_example_catalog.md"],"answer_summary":"Single-agent sufficiency wins explicitly. The active runtime bundle says to minimize unnecessary orchestration, keep one coherent path when sufficient, avoid forcing A2A, and keep guide-reflection maintenance primarily document-grounded unless stronger behavior-proof evidence is actually needed.","verdict_rationale":"The active bundle provides a direct runtime-owned answer path. `snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/AGENTS.md` says to minimize unnecessary orchestration, prefer one coherent path over decorative orchestration, and choose the lowest maturity surface that safely solves the task. `snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/01_base/PROMPT_full.md` keeps `single-agent vs multi-agent` as an explicit routing choice, says direct solve should win when reliable, and marks decorative delegation as an anti-pattern. `snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/02_overlays/PROMPT_multi_agent_overlay.md` says not to activate multi-agent when one coherent agent path is sufficient, not to force A2A when a simpler direct path is sufficient, and that collaboration must earn its coordination cost. `snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/codex/skills/orchestration-control/SKILL.md` requires `Check Single-Agent Sufficiency` first and says orchestration should be chosen only on material control gain, otherwise collapse back to the simpler route. For the pending guide-reflection maintenance task, `snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/codex/CODEX_RUNTIME_GUIDE.md` gives a direct reroute: stay at document-level benchmark for doc/runtime parity and benchmark coverage, and escalate to assembled replay only when ambiguity, conflict, or behavior-proof needs make document review insufficient. The same guide also says that if a chapter family can only be answered by operator-facing prose, that is a runtime reflection gap rather than a valid answer surface. `snapshot/prompt-stack/v32/03_examples/PROMPT_example_catalog.md` reinforces that operator-only docs are excluded as the runtime answer surface and that guide-reflection review remains document-grounded until the task actually moves into assembled replay. The same active bundle also prefers narrow diffs, local edits, and smallest-invalid-unit recovery, so there is no evidence of speculative patch widening. Stronger operational artifacts are not missing here because the runtime guide and example catalog already expose the replay-escalation route and its stronger artifacts for the cases that truly need them.","limitations":["This verdict is limited to the listed active bundle files and judges orchestration sufficiency and route ownership, not the downstream contents of any non-active rerouted skill bundle.","No live benchmark execution, assembled replay run, or runner-state artifact was reviewed; this is a document-level route sufficiency verdict, not a behavior-proof verdict."]}
